    "product_id": "591a4f52-9421-4451-b9ce-4500de7a6a35",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 010455\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n955 PM MST Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Aside from a few showers possible in far eastern Gila County this \nafternoon, dry conditions with near zero rain chances will persist \nthe next several days.\n\n- As high pressure gradually builds over the region, a gradual\nwarming trend is expected with near to slightly above normal \ntemperatures returning by the end of the week and into the weekend. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest water vapor satellite imagery indicated the upper low \nresponsible for the active weather the last couple of days is \ncurrently centered over NM. Much of the area is currently on the \nsubsident side of the low with drier air building in. A few showers, \nhowever, may still develop mainly across far eastern Gila County \nthis afternoon along the deformation zone on the back side of the \nlow. Otherwise, with the drier air and ridging aloft gradually \nbuilding in from the eastern Pacific, dry conditions with zero rain \nchances will prevail through the rest of the week under mostly clear \nskies. \n\nDue to the lingering influences of the negative 500 mb height \nanomalies from the departing upper low, afternoon high temperatures \ntoday across much of the south-central AZ lower deserts will only \ntop out in the low 80s, which is a solid 10 degrees below normal for \nthis time of the year, while warmer readings in the 90s can be \nexpected across the western deserts. As the ridging aloft gradually \nbuilds over the region during the next couple of days, temperatures \nthrough the end of the week will gradually warm up with lower desert \nhighs returning to the mid 90s across south-central AZ to around 100 \ndegrees along and west of the Lower Colorado River Valley by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nModel guidance shows upper-lvl ridging migrating over the Four \nCorners region by this weekend with 500 mb hght fields increasing to \naround 588-590 dam. As this occurs, global models show a weak upper- \nlow developing over N MX and far W TX which will induce easterly \nflow aloft over our forecast area. The increased pressure gradient \nbetween both features will bring breezy conditions to southeastern \nand southcentral AZ from Saturday through Monday. Elevated winds \nwill likely develop each morning and persist into the early \nafternoon with gusts reaching 30-35 mph over the high terrain and 20-\n 25 mph across the lower deserts. This sfc easterly flow will help \nkeep high temperatures near normal across southcentral AZ, but \novernight lows will be elevated in the low to mid 70s. The 500 mb \nridge axis looks to shift back to the over SoCal and the Great Basin \nearly next week as a weak trough passes through the Intermountain \nWest. This pattern should maintain temperatures in a near normal \nrange across central and eastern portions of the region, but allow \nhighs to warm around 3-5 degrees above normal across the western \ndeserts where highs will likely reach the low 100s each afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0455Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nUncertainty in wind directions and timing of wind shifts will be the \nmain weather issues through Thursday night under clear skies. \nConfidence is excellent that east winds will prevail into at least \nThursday afternoon before significant uncertainty arises. Winds may \nattempt to become W/NW late afternoon, however may only be able to \nachieve a variable direction before reverting back to easterly mid \nevening. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather issues will exist through Thursday night under clear \nskies. While light S/SE winds will more common through the period, \nlight and variable winds will prevail for prolonged stretches. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAside from a few afternoon showers across the far eastern districts \ntoday, dry conditions will prevail during the next several days. \nTemperatures will remain below normal across south-central AZ and \nnear normal for areas along and west of the Colorado River Valley \nthrough the rest of this week with gradual warming anticipated \nthrough the weekend. Relative humidity will bottom out around 15-30% \nacross the western districts and remain above 40% across the eastern \ndistricts through the end of the week along with good to excellent \novernight recoveries. Winds will be light and diurnal with speeds \nremaining predominantly 10 mph or less over the next few days before \na stronger easterly gradient wind sets in by this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Salerno\n",
